    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Nearly every headline melanocortin drug switches a receptor <em>on</em>. The quieter half
    of the field switches one <em>off</em>. Antagonists matter because several diseases are
    driven by too much melanocortin tone — appetite jammed shut in{' '}
    <a href="/cachexia">cachexia</a>, adrenal androgens roaring under excess ACTH — and because
    the body already runs on natural brakes. This is the map of them: the endogenous blockers,
    the research tools that proved the biology, and the clinical antagonists now in
    trials.<Cite n={1} />
  </p>

  <h2>What "antagonist" means here</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>antagonist</strong> binds a receptor and blocks it, so the natural signal cannot
    get through. A <em>neutral</em> antagonist occupies the site without changing the receptor’s
    baseline activity; an <em>inverse</em> agonist goes further and suppresses constitutive
    signalling. At melanocortin receptors the distinction is real: Pfizer’s{' '}
    <a href="/pf-07258669">PF-07258669</a> was designed as a neutral MC4R antagonist, while the
    body’s own <a href="/agrp">AgRP</a> behaves as an inverse agonist at MC4R.<Cite n={3} /><Cite n={5} />
    The counterpart page — the drugs that turn the system on — is{' '}
    <a href="/melanocortin-agonists">melanocortin agonists</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The body makes its own brakes</h2>
  <p>
    Two peptides, from genes outside <a href="/pomc">POMC</a>, are the system’s natural
    antagonists:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/agrp">AgRP</a></strong> — expressed in the arcuate nucleus; blocks
      MC3R and MC4R and drives hunger. It is the endogenous reason an MC4R antagonist can
      restore appetite.<Cite n={3} />
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><a href="/asip">ASIP</a> (agouti signalling protein)</strong> — the pigment
      off-switch; blocks <a href="/receptors/mc1r">MC1R</a> and tips melanin production from
      dark eumelanin toward lighter pheomelanin.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Together they are the proof that antagonism is not a drug-company invention — it is how the
    system is wired.
  </p>

  <h2>The classic research tool: SHU9119</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/shu9119">SHU9119</a> is the reference melanocortin antagonist: a cyclic peptide
    from the same Arizona chemistry that produced Melanotan II, flipped into a blocker by a
    bulky D-Nal substitution at position 7.<Cite n={2} /> It antagonises MC3R and MC4R, is a
    partial agonist at MC5R, and is an agonist at MC1R — which is why it can silence the
    appetite receptor while leaving the pigment receptor on. Injected into the brain it makes
    animals eat. That experiment is the foundation under every later MC4R-antagonist drug.
  </p>

  <h2>Clinical antagonists — two diseases of excess</h2>
  <p>
    The therapeutic antagonists cluster around two problems where the system is over-driven:
  </p>
  <div class="not-prose my-8 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-sm">
