  <h3>Cachexia — MC4R in reverse</h3>
  <p>
    In <a href="/cachexia">cachexia</a>, inflammatory signals over-activate central MC4R and jam
    the appetite rheostat toward “stop eating and burn.” An MC4R antagonist lifts that brake.{' '}
    <a href="/tcmcb07">TCMCB07</a> (mifomelatide) is the peptide in Phase 2 for cancer
    cachexia — effectively a drug version of AgRP’s job.<Cite n={4} />{' '}
    <a href="/pf-07258669">PF-07258669</a> was Pfizer’s oral small-molecule attempt at the same
    target: potent, selective, structure-designed, efficacious in aged-rat cachexia, then
    discontinued in Phase 1 for a business decision rather than a safety concern.<Cite n={5} />
  </p>

  <h3>Adrenal disease — the first MC2R blocker</h3>
  <p>
    Atumelnant (CRN04894) is the clearest clinical example of blocking a melanocortin receptor
    as a therapeutic strategy. It is an oral <a href="/receptors/mc2r">MC2R</a> antagonist aimed
    at congenital adrenal hyperplasia and ACTH-dependent Cushing’s — conditions driven by too
    much ACTH signalling at the adrenal. In Phase 2 it cut adrenal androgen precursors
    substantially and has moved into pivotal Phase 3 work.<Cite n={6} /> Until it earns its own
    page, the mechanism lives on the MC2R receptor page and in the{' '}
    <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a> table. It is also the antagonist that balances the site’s
    agonist-heavy framing: melanocortin medicine is not only “turn it on.”
  </p>

  <h2>The honest bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    Antagonists are the smaller, earlier half of melanocortin pharmacology — and the half that
    makes the system bidirectional. Natural brakes (AgRP, ASIP), a definitive research tool
    (SHU9119), cachexia programmes that run MC4R backwards, and an MC2R blocker in Phase 3 for
    adrenal disease: that is the off-switch ledger. Read it next to the{' '}
    <a href="/melanocortin-agonists">agonists hub</a>, and track status on the{' '}
    <a href="/pipeline">pipeline</a> and <a href="/therapeutics">therapeutics</a> pages.
  </p>

